// **a `const` variable, which is not a compile-time constant.** It has storage, it lives in whatever scope
// it was written in - including a block - and it carries a `$` like every other value in the language. A
// constant is the `$`-less form, declared at file, namespace or struct scope; see const/ for those.
//
// this form parsed from the day `const` did and was never pinned by anything. It is here so the two cannot
// be confused for one change: nothing below may move when constants arrive.

const usize $max = 100;

usize $n = $max;
echo $n;

// inferred, which is the other spelling: `const $x = ...` takes its type from the initializer and applies
// the const bit to it
const $limit = 42;
echo $limit;

// and a block-local one, which is exactly what a compile-time constant may *not* be - the copy a constant
// makes at each use site has no scope to be local to
if (1 == 1) {
    const $inner = 7;
    echo $inner;
}
